    10→lang: "py"
    11→
    12→description: |
    13→  
    14→  You will implement a custom fp8-blockwise matmul kernel optimized for MI300.
    15→  You will be given single-precision scaling factors for your matrices.
    16→  The shapes of all outer and inner dimensions of tensors are from DeepSeek-R1.
    17→  To be explicit, you will be given a tuple of tensors:
    18→  ```
    19→  (a, b, a_scale, b_scale, c)
    20→  ```
    21→  where `a` and `b` are the input matrices, `a_scale` and `b_scale` are the scaling factors for `a` and `b` respectively,
    22→  and `c` is the output matrix:
    23→  * `a` is M x K in column-major order in e4m3fnuz
    24→  * `b` is N x K in column-major order in e4m3fnuz
    25→  * `a_scale` is M x K // 128 in column-major order in fp32
    26→  * `b_scale` is N // 128 x K // 128 in column-major order in fp32
    27→  * `c` is M x N in ROW-major order in bf16
    28→  
    29→  Matrix sizes `m` and `n` are divisible by 64, `k` is divisible by 128.
    30→
    31→  The ranking criteria is the geometric mean of the benchmark results.
    32→
    33→  For the grand price, your kernel will be evaluated against the speed of light analysis
    34→  and the solution closest to the speed of light will be awarded the grand price.
    35→  ```
    36→  The speed of light analysis is:
    37→   M       N       K     time[us]
    38→  1024    1536    7168      8.63
    39→  1024    4608    7168     25.89
    40→  6144    1536    7168     51.78
    41→  6144    4608    7168    155.30
    42→  1024    7168     256      3.17
    43→  6144    7168     256     17.27
    44→  ```
    45→
